WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.027
Dive profile tabs, part two: reading the Partial Pressure tab.

00:00:05.177 --> 00:00:10.476
Click Partial Pressure and go full screen.

00:00:10.626 --> 00:00:18.780
Each gas in the mix carries its share of the ambient pressure: green is oxygen, purple is nitrogen, on the right axis.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:31.482
On the surface, air gives 0.21 bar of oxygen. At 40 m it is five times more, 1.05 bar, below the 1.4 bar bottom limit.

00:00:31.632 --> 00:00:42.437
Nitrogen reaches 3.96 bar at 40 m, just under the 4 bar narcosis line: mild narcosis is already possible on air at this depth.

00:00:42.587 --> 00:00:54.938
At 21 m you switch to EAN50: oxygen jumps to 1.56 bar, just under the 1.6 bar deco limit, and nitrogen drops.

00:00:55.088 --> 00:01:08.192
Breathing less nitrogen speeds up off-gassing; that is what the deco gas is for. In EAN50 oxygen and nitrogen are equal, so the green line hides under the purple one as they step down together.

00:01:08.342 --> 00:01:15.122
After surfacing you breathe air again: oxygen is back at 0.21 bar.

00:01:15.272 --> 00:01:23.848
The limits drawn, 1.4 and 1.6 bar, are common teaching values; follow the limits of your own training agency.

00:01:23.998 --> 00:01:27.858
Next: gas consumption. Thanks for watching!
